{"author":{"id":"c7109691837125ce179ae67c00dda2acb755e6dfb3646ed3e9aad234d639bbcc"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1775665221,"nonce":"4cLBKEesS50LCc3/XR06Lbe4O+c=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs7748MT","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/23","origin":"github"},"title":"Fix time handling for `domain.variable(\"initial\")` and local AD evaluation in time-dependent DeepONet workflows","message":"### Title\nFix time handling for `domain.variable(\"initial\")` and local AD evaluation in time-dependent DeepONet workflows\n\n###Sumamry\nWhen using jNO for time-dependent battery modeling with DeepONet, the initial slice could not be used cleanly through `domain.variable(\"initial\")`. The API returned the initial spatial coordinates, but not a matching local time variable for `t=t0`, which forced manual workarounds such as creating a separate `initial_time` tensor.\n\nIn addition, the trace evaluation path for automatic differentiation did not handle temporal variables robustly when mixed with sampled spatial coordinates and parametric operator inputs such as `GRF`. This led to shape mismatches during `concat([r, t])`, incorrect local slicing behavior in Jacobian evaluation, and inference/training failures in time-dependent operator-learning examples.\n\n### Root cause\nThe issue came from a combination of API and evaluator limitations:\n\n1. `domain.variable(\"initial\")` only exposed the initial spatial set, but not a matching initial time variable.\n2. Temporal variable evaluation relied only on the shared `__time__` tag and did not properly support local fixed-time tags.\n3. Local AD contexts in `TraceEvaluator` did not reliably distinguish between:\n   - sampled spatial point sets\n   - parametric tensor/operator inputs such as `GRF`\n4. Temporal broadcasting in local Jacobian evaluation could use the wrong active point count, causing time coordinates to be expanded against incompatible spatial shapes.","files":null}]}